From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 19:13:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] D9b: undoing a statement's import reverses the alert upgrades
 it made
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A statement row that matched an alert's transaction by bank reference
upgraded it in place (D4): cleared it, filled its memo with the reference
and its raw fields. That created nothing, so undoBatch — which removes what
a batch created and reverses what it merged (IMPORTS-6) — never saw it, and
the undone statement went on vouching for the transaction.

The upgrade's event now names its batch (upgraded_by_batch_id in the after
state; the row keeps its own import_batch_id). undoBatch finds those events
and puts the before-state's cleared, memo and raw fields back, breaking any
checkpoint behind a transaction it un-clears. A transaction changed since
the upgrade — a later event, or the upgraded columns differing — is left
and listed with the edited records, as an edited merge is. Upgrades made
before this change carry no batch id and are not reversed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
